\label{tab:artifact_availability}
\end{table}

\textbf{Dataset Curation.} \dataset{} is designed to compare accepts and rejects under the same paper artifact. Existing open-source datasets often violate this. DeepReview mixes arXiv LaTeX with PDF-to-Markdown extraction, introducing format shortcuts such as different equation syntax; in our audit, 78.74\% of 2024 papers followed LaTeX-style equation markup, whereas 52.97\% of 2025 papers followed PDF-extracted formatting. Sakana's ICLR 2022 dataset is more extreme: accepted papers are camera-ready while rejected papers are rebuttal versions, so patterns such as `anonymous', `double blind', non-anonymized GitHub links, acknowledgements, and paper length become label-correlated. Deep Paper Gestalt \citep{huang2018deeppapergestalt} introduces a different mismatch by approximating rejects with workshop papers. By matching accepts and rejects to the latest rebuttal artifact, \dataset{} removes the strongest accept/reject artifact mismatch. Real structural differences still remain, however: accepted papers tend to be longer, contain more content, and more often include open-science GitHub links, even after appendix removal and artifact normalization.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/dataset_overview.pdf}
\caption{\textbf{Dataset composition and residual structure.} \textbf{(a)} Annual accept/reject distribution across ICLR years used in \dataset{}. \textbf{(b)} Structural differences between accepts and rejects: token count, page count, figure images, and cited references by year.}
\label{fig:dataset_overview}
\end{figure*}

\textbf{Modalities.} We study two paper representations: \textit{text} and \textit{vision}. Both are derived from the same latest-rebuttal PDF. We parse each PDF with MinerU \citep{wang2024mineru}, use its structured labels and positional metadata to remove headers, asides such as line numbering, and all content in and after the references section, and then produce aligned text and vision views. For text, excluded content is simply omitted from the markdown serialization. For vision, the same regions are whitened directly on the rendered pages, including truncation of all material from the start of references onward on the final retained page. This keeps the text and vision representations as uniform as possible while preserving the actual content reviewers judge. We also experimented with several normalization procedures for ICLR 2024, but residual layout cues remained strong enough that the vision model could exploit them; we defer those diagnostics to the appendix, which is why 2024 is excluded.

\section{Methods}
To learn the final decision label, we compare three strategies: inference-time prompting, supervised fine-tuning (SFT), and reinforcement learning (RL). Learning-based experiments use Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct for text and Qwen2.5-VL-7B-Instruct for vision \citep{bai2025qwen2}, since these models predate ICLR 2025 and ICLR 2026 and better evaluate the effects of our training, rather than the information in their pretraining data.

\paragraph{Base Models and Temporal Cutoffs.} Inference-only baselines additionally include Gemini-2.5-Flash \citep{comanici2025gemini} and GPT-5.4 high reasoning, within the Codex scaffold. We tested Codex because papers are long, and programatically parsing the paper could be more efficient, similar in spirit to recursive language-models \citep{zhang2026recursivelanguagemodels}. Gemini's January 2025 cutoff places ICLR 2025 largely out of scope, GPT-5.4's August 31, 2025 cutoff may overlap with ICLR 2025 but not ICLR 2026, so ICLR 2026 is therefore the cleanest out-of-cutoff setting for all closed-source baselines.

\paragraph{Non-Neural Baselines.} We include two simple baselines that require no language model. \textbf{TF-IDF + Logistic Regression} vectorizes the full paper text with a 500-feature TF-IDF representation and fits a logistic regression classifier. \textbf{Tabular Logistic Regression} extracts 187 hand-crafted features from paper metadata (full list in the Appendix), then fits a logistic regression over the standardized features.

\paragraph{Inference-Time Prompting.} Inference-time baselines operate without weight updates: given a single paper, the model produces a review-style response and returns a final decision label. Prompt variants, including critical prompts and few-shot settings, are deferred to \Cref{inf_results}.

\paragraph{Supervised Fine-Tuning.} For SFT, we fine-tune Qwen2.5 and Qwen2.5-VL 7B directly on \dataset{} using LlamaFactory \citep{zheng2024llamafactoryunifiedefficientfinetuning}. We also test data-mixture variants designed to improve generalization on ICLR 2025 and ICLR 2026.

\paragraph{Reinforcement Learning.} Our RL experiments use GRPO-style training following DeepSeek-R1 \citep{Guo_2025}. We study single-turn text RL, four-turn text RL with temporally filtered arXiv access, and single-turn vision RL. To avoid look-ahead bias, retrieval is restricted to results predating the relevant submission cycle, e.g. for ICLR 2025 submissions all arXiv results must predate December 15, 2024.

\section{Results}

Table~\ref{tab:main_results} summarizes the paper's main empirical result. Supervised fine-tuning on \dataset{} is the strongest regime overall, with our best vision model reaching $70.9\%$ on ICLR 2025 and $66.6\%$ on ICLR 2026. In contrast, inference-only baselines remain poorly calibrated and reinforcement learning improves over prompting but does not close the gap to SFT, making learned multimodal decision models the central result of the paper.
