\paragraph{Inference-Time Prompting} \label{inf_results}

As our baselines, we analyze the ability of base LLMs on our dataset. We find that across open and closed models, naive prompting results in near-100\% acceptance rate, resulting in high accept recall but poor reject recall. We therefore explored prompt variants that tried to make the models more critical and better calibrated, but these changes were not enough to turn frontier base models into reliable acceptance predictors. We evaluated critical-analysis language, structured review generation, free-form review generation, and two-shot prompting with one accept and one reject in context. These interventions could often reduce the predicted acceptance rate toward $50\%$, but the resulting decision accuracy remained weak: models became less acceptance-biased without becoming substantially better judges of paper quality. The best Qwen run achieves an accuracy of 52.84\% and the best Gemini inference strategy achieved 54.11\%, which are respectively 2.66\% and 0.91\% higher than the naive prompting method.This failure is what motivated the SFT and RL investigations.

\paragraph{Non-Neural Baselines}\label{non_neural_results}

