We include two non-neural baselines to contextualize the neural results. TF-IDF + logistic regression reaches 61.1\% on ICLR 2025 and 59.9\% on ICLR 2026, suggesting that surface-level word frequencies carry limited signal for acceptance prediction. The stronger tabular logistic baseline does substantially better in-distribution, reaching 67.2\% on ICLR 2025, but degrades to 61.4\% on ICLR 2026.

The accept-predictive features paint a consistent picture of \emph{thoroughness}: accepted papers have larger appendices, more references, denser internal cross-references (e.g., ``see Figure 3'', ``as in Equation 5''), more inline mathematics, and are more likely to mention ablation studies. The most influential logistic-regression features are summarized in Table~\ref{tab:baseline_features}. The reject-predictive side is dominated by \emph{submission year}, reflecting increasing competition over time rather than paper quality. Other reject signals include longer titles, higher density of long or unusual words, and mention of Graph Neural Networks (GNN).

\subsection{Supervised Fine-Tuning}\label{SFT_results}

Relative to prompting, SFT is the first approach that improves both accept and reject recall at once, reaching $70.9\%$ on ICLR 2025 and $66.6\%$ on ICLR 2026 for our best vision model. For evaluation, we decode greedily at temperature 0 and compute confidence from the resulting decision-token log-probability. Restricting to predictions with confidence at least $0.8$ raises accuracy to $80.3\%$ while retaining roughly a third of the balanced ICLR 2020--2026 test set.

\begin{figure*}[h!]
      \centering
      \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/calibration_1x3.pdf}
      \caption{\textbf{Calibration and quality-signal analysis.} (a)~After Platt scaling, both the text and vision models are reasonably well
  calibrated: confidence tracks empirical accuracy, and filtering to predictions with $\mathrm{conf}\geq 0.7$ yields roughly $80\%$ accuracy on
  about $30\%$ of the test set. (b)~Pearson correlation between quality signals and citation percentile on ICLR~2025; black dots are binned
  empirical means and colored lines are linear fits. PaperLens-Text $p(\mathrm{accept})$ ($r{=}0.30$) slightly exceeds \textit{Pctl.~Rating}
  ($r{=}0.26$) at predicting citation impact. (c)~Vision-model accuracy surface over raw confidence and percentile rating. Accuracy is highest in
  a low-rating, high-confidence region ($96\%$) and also rises again in the high-confidence, high-rating region ($85\%$), while the borderline
  region remains less reliable.}
      \label{fig:calibration}
  \end{figure*}

\paragraph{Calibration and Selective Reliability.}
After Platt scaling (Appendix~\ref{appendix:calibration}), both SFT models are well calibrated: predicted confidence closely tracks empirical accuracy (Figure~\ref{fig:calibration}a). The key property is \emph{selective reliability}---practitioners can trade coverage for accuracy by thresholding on confidence. At ${\geq}0.7$, the vision model reaches $80.3\%$ accuracy on $34\%$ of test papers; at ${\geq}0.8$, $90.4\%$ on $14\%$; at ${\geq}0.9$, $96.2\%$ on $6\%$. Figure~\ref{fig:calibration}c shows this reliability concentrates at quality extremes: accuracy peaks for strong accepts and clear rejects but troughs in the borderline region (percentile rating $0.4$--$0.6$), where human reviewers disagree.

\paragraph{Citation-Impact Correlation.}
To test whether the model captures genuine quality signals beyond binary decisions, we correlate each quality signal with citation impact on ICLR~2025 (Figure~\ref{fig:calibration}b). We define \emph{percentile rating} as the within-year percentile rank of the mean reviewer score (average-rank normalized to handle cross-year scale differences) and \emph{percentile citation} as the within-year percentile rank of cumulative citations as of December~2025 (one year of citation data for ICLR~2025 submissions). The text model's $p(\text{accept})$ correlates with citation percentile at $r{=}0.30$---\emph{exceeding} the correlation between human reviewer ratings and citations ($r{=}0.26$). Vision reaches $r{=}0.25$, roughly matching human ratings. A model trained only on binary accept/reject labels, with no access to reviewer scores or citation data, thus learns quality signals that predict real-world impact at or above the level of humans.

\paragraph{Data Mixture.}

