    \caption{\textbf{Mean reviewer rating by decision and year.} Gap = accept $-$ reject. ICLR 2026 has the smallest gap.}
    \label{tab:rating_gap}
    \vspace{-1.5em}
\end{wraptable}

Figure~\ref{fig:data-mixture}a--b summarizes the data-mixture ablations. Training on a balanced set of ICLR 2020--2023 and 2025 produces competitive ICLR~2025 accuracy, and \emph{rating-agreeing} filtering---which retains only accepts with percentile rating ${\in}[0.6,1]$ and rejects with ${\in}[0,0.4]$, discarding ${\sim}14\%$ of borderline training samples---improves 2025 further by sharpening the decision boundary. However, neither configuration generalizes to ICLR~2026. Rating-agreeing filtering actually \emph{hurts} 2026, because the accept/reject rating gap in 2026 is at an all-time low (Table~\ref{tab:rating_gap}, gap${=}1.44$): most papers are borderline, so removing borderline cases discards the very signal the model needs. Only including ICLR~2026 data directly into training (highlighted in Figure~\ref{fig:data-mixture}a--b) closes this gap, yielding the strongest 2026 results. We also tested appending synthetic reviews generated by Qwen3.5-120B and Gemini-2.5-Flash, but these did not consistently improve accuracy across years or modalities---the simplest paper-only mixture wins. Additional ablations (paper-statistics features, thinking tokens, multimodal fusion) are reported in Appendix~\ref{app:data_mixture}.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/data_mixture_combined.pdf}
\caption{\textbf{Data mixture and cross-conference generalization.} (a,\,b)~Effect of training configuration on SFT accuracy for ICLR 2025 and 2026; text (blue) and vision (orange) shown side by side. Rating-agreeing filtering improves 2025 but hurts 2026; adding 2026 data (green highlight) yields the strongest 2026 results. (c)~Cross-conference accuracy on balanced NeurIPS/ICML evaluation sets. Midtraining on all-venue papers before ICLR-only SFT consistently improves accuracy, suggesting the cross-conference gap is driven by format unfamiliarity rather than the decision task.}
\label{fig:data-mixture}
\end{figure*}

\paragraph{Cross-Conference Generalization.}
Although \dataset{} trains exclusively on ICLR decisions, we test whether the learned decision model transfers to NeurIPS and ICML. Since rejected papers at these venues are only public through opt-in release, we construct balanced evaluation sets by downsampling accepts to match the available reject count: NeurIPS~2024 (392 papers), NeurIPS~2025 (494), and ICML~2025 (316). Both the text and vision SFT models degrade under this setting, which we attribute to \emph{format surprise}---papers at different venues follow subtly different stylistic conventions unseen during fine-tuning.

To bridge this gap, we add a \textbf{midtraining} stage: next-token prediction on the full text of papers from NeurIPS (${\sim}18{,}100$ papers, 2021--2025) and ICML (${\sim}6{,}500$ papers, 2024--2025), using all available splits with no decision labels. This lightweight language-modeling pass exposes the model to target-venue writing styles without any accept/reject signal. As Figure~\ref{fig:data-mixture}c shows, midtraining consistently improves accuracy: NeurIPS~2025 rises from $69.0\%$ to $71.5\%$, ICML~2025 from $67.4\%$ to $69.9\%$, and NeurIPS~2024 from $65.3\%$ to $66.6\%$. The result confirms that cross-conference generalization is blocked more by distributional mismatch than by the decision criterion itself.

\section{Discussion}

Chain-of-thought reviewing by frontier LLMs produces detailed, structured reviews but struggles with calibration: without external grounding, the model's reasoning path tends to over-accept. Figure~\ref{fig:cot_dag} visualizes this as a directed acyclic graph: the CoT process first assesses novelty, then execution quality, before reaching a decision. Crucially, the distribution over reasoning traces is conditioned on the eventual decision---$p(\text{CoT} \mid \text{decision}, \text{paper})$---so accept-path traces (``Novel claim $\to$ Good experiments'') and reject-path traces (``Incremental $\to$ Weak experiments'') diverge early. An uncalibrated model that defaults to acceptance will cascade through the wrong reasoning branch regardless of the paper's actual quality.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.48\textwidth}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/cot_dag.pdf}
\captionof{figure}{\textbf{Decision-conditioned CoT paths.} The review reasoning process follows a DAG where $p(\text{CoT} \mid \text{decision}, \text{paper})$ determines which branch is taken. Accept paths (green) favor ``Novel claim $\to$ Good experiments''; reject paths (red) favor ``Incremental $\to$ Weak experiments.'' Supplying a calibrated SFT prior effectively conditions on the likely decision at the root, steering the entire downstream reasoning toward the appropriate branch.}
\label{fig:cot_dag}
\end{minipage}
\hfill
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.50\textwidth}
\centering
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3.5pt}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.15}
