\label{tab:agent_results}
\vspace{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{l|ccc|cccc|c}
\toprule
& \multicolumn{3}{c|}{\textbf{Acc.\,(\%)}} & \multicolumn{4}{c|}{\textbf{Alignment (1--5)}} & \textbf{Judge} \\
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{'25} & \textbf{'26} & \textbf{All} & \textbf{Wk.} & \textbf{Nov.} & \textbf{Rat.} & \textbf{Ql.} & \textbf{Mean} \\
\midrule
SFT Vision & 72.0 & 67.0 & 69.5 & -- & -- & -- & -- & -- \\
Claude & 57.5 & 70.0 & 63.7 & 4.01 & 3.70 & 3.55 & 3.79 & 4.00 \\
\,\,+ SFT & \textbf{70.5} & \textbf{71.5} & \textbf{71.0} & \cellcolor{green!15}\textbf{4.30} & \cellcolor{green!15}\textbf{3.89} & 3.46 & \cellcolor{green!15}\textbf{3.92} & \cellcolor{green!15}\textbf{4.11} \\
\midrule
$\Delta$ & \textcolor{green!50!black}{+13} & \textcolor{green!50!black}{+1.5} & \textcolor{green!50!black}{+7.3} & \textcolor{green!50!black}{+.29} & \textcolor{green!50!black}{+.20} & {--.08} & \textcolor{green!50!black}{+.13} & \textcolor{green!50!black}{+.11} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{minipage}
\end{figure*}

We test whether conditioning the LLM reviewer on predictions from our calibrated SFT vision model can correct this bias. We deploy Claude Code (Opus) as an autonomous reviewer on 400 ICLR papers (200 from 2025, 200 from 2026; 30\% accept / 70\% reject, matching the natural acceptance rate). Each paper receives a full structured review with 11 fields, produced by a sub-agent that reads the paper content and page images. We compare two conditions: (1)~\textbf{Claude}: the LLM reviews papers with no external signal; (2)~\textbf{Claude + SFT}: each paper additionally includes the SFT vision model's prediction and confidence as a Bayesian prior. Review quality is evaluated by an independent LLM judge that compares agent reviews against real ICLR human reviews on five alignment dimensions (1--5 scale each).

Table~\ref{tab:agent_results} reveals a striking pattern. The SFT vision model alone achieves 69.5\% accuracy as a binary classifier. Claude Code \emph{without} the model prior performs \emph{worse} at 63.7\%, despite producing detailed chain-of-thought reviews. The gap is largest on ICLR~2025 (57.5\% vs.\ 72.0\%), where the uncalibrated agent accepts 83\% of papers in its worst batch. This confirms the CoT accept bias illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:cot_dag}: without calibration, the reasoning process generates plausible-sounding justifications for acceptance even when the paper is weak, because it defaults to the accept branch of the DAG.

Conditioning on the SFT prior closes this gap entirely. Claude + SFT achieves 71.0\% overall---exceeding the SFT model alone---and the improvement is concentrated on ICLR~2025 (+13.0 percentage points), precisely where the uncalibrated agent struggles most. The prior acts as an anchor at the root of the CoT DAG: when the SFT model signals ``reject'' with high confidence, the agent's reasoning is steered toward the reject branch, causing it to identify specific weaknesses rather than defaulting to acceptance.

\paragraph{Decision Transitions.} Table~\ref{tab:transitions} quantifies how the SFT prior changes decisions. Overall, 79.2\% of decisions remain unchanged (71.9\% accuracy on these). Of the 20.8\% that flip, the dominant direction is Accept$\to$Reject (14.0\%), which is correct 76.8\% of the time---the prior successfully overrides false acceptances. The reverse flip (Reject$\to$Accept) is rarer (6.8\%) and less reliable (48.1\% correct), consistent with the model's reject bias. On ICLR~2025, where the uncalibrated agent is most biased, 25\% of decisions flip and 76\% of those flips are correct.

