\caption{\textbf{Decision transitions when adding the SFT prior.} When the SFT prior changes the agent's decision, the dominant direction is Accept$\to$Reject (14\% of all papers), and these flips are correct 76.8\% of the time---confirming that the prior primarily corrects false acceptances. The effect is strongest on ICLR~2025 (81.0\% flip accuracy), where uncalibrated CoT over-acceptance is most severe.}
\label{tab:transitions}
\end{table}

The alignment scores in Table~\ref{tab:agent_results} reveal that the prior improves not just the binary decision but the \emph{substance} of reviews. Weakness identification improves by +0.29 (4.01 $\to$ 4.30): conditioned on the correct branch, the agent more reliably identifies the primary weakness flagged by human reviewers. Novel concerns improve by +0.20 (3.70 $\to$ 3.89): the agent raises more valid points not mentioned in human reviews. Overall review quality improves by +0.13 (3.79 $\to$ 3.92).

\paragraph{Qualitative Examples.} Figures~\ref{fig:review_example_1}--\ref{fig:review_example_10} present the top 10 papers where the SFT prior corrects the agent's decision, showing side-by-side excerpts from the human review, uncalibrated agent review, and SFT-conditioned agent review. Each example includes the summary, weakness, and strength fields to illustrate how the prior affects multiple aspects of the review simultaneously. Examples 1--5 show Accept$\to$Reject corrections; Examples 6--10 show Reject$\to$Accept corrections.

