\appendix

\section{Post-Hoc Calibration Details}\label{app:calibration}

Modern neural networks are often poorly calibrated, producing confidence estimates that do not reflect true correctness probabilities~\citep{Guo_2017}. We apply post-hoc calibration to our SFT models using two standard methods: temperature scaling and Platt scaling.

\paragraph{Expected Calibration Error.}
Following \citet{Guo_2017}, we partition predictions into $M$ equal-width confidence bins $B_1, \ldots, B_M$ and define the \emph{expected calibration error} (ECE) as
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{ECE} = \sum_{m=1}^{M} \frac{|B_m|}{n} \left| \mathrm{acc}(B_m) - \mathrm{conf}(B_m) \right|,
\end{equation}
where $\mathrm{acc}(B_m) = \frac{1}{|B_m|}\sum_{i \in B_m} \mathbf{1}[\hat{y}_i = y_i]$ is the empirical accuracy and $\mathrm{conf}(B_m) = \frac{1}{|B_m|}\sum_{i \in B_m} p_i$ is the mean predicted confidence in bin $B_m$. A perfectly calibrated model has $\mathrm{ECE} = 0$.

\paragraph{Raw Calibration.}
The text model is well-calibrated out of the box (ECE $= 0.028$), while the vision model is overconfident: its reliability curve lies below the diagonal in Figure~\ref{fig:calibration_val}, yielding ECE $= 0.042$.

\paragraph{Temperature Scaling.}
Temperature scaling~\citep{Guo_2017} uses a single parameter $T > 0$. Given the model's raw decision-token logprob, we compute the logit $z = \log(p / (1 - p))$ and rescale:
\begin{equation}
p_{\mathrm{cal}} = \sigma(z / T),
\end{equation}
where $\sigma$ is the sigmoid function. $T > 1$ softens overconfident predictions toward $0.5$; $T < 1$ sharpens. We fit $T$ by minimizing the negative log-likelihood (NLL) on the validation set. For text, $T = 1.016$ (essentially unchanged, confirming that the text model is already well calibrated). For vision, $T = 1.475$ (significant softening, correcting the overconfidence).

\paragraph{Platt Scaling.}
Platt scaling~\citep{Platt_1999} generalizes temperature scaling with two parameters $(a, b)$:
\begin{equation}
p_{\mathrm{cal}} = \sigma(a \cdot z + b).
\end{equation}
Temperature scaling is the special case $a = 1/T$, $b = 0$. The additional bias $b$ allows shifting the decision boundary, correcting systematic prediction bias (e.g., a tendency to over-accept) that pure temperature rescaling cannot address.

Fitted parameters: text $a = 0.845$, $b = 0.147$ (slight compression with a small upward shift); vision $a = 0.636$, $b = 0.062$ (strong compression, equivalent to $T \approx 1.57$, with minimal shift).

\paragraph{Why Platt Scaling Outperforms Temperature Scaling.}
The NLL of a probabilistic classifier decomposes into calibration loss, refinement loss, and entropy~\citep{DeGroot_1983}. Temperature and Platt scaling have only 1--2 parameters, so they cannot meaningfully alter prediction rankings (refinement). Consequently, minimizing NLL with these constrained models reduces almost exclusively the calibration term. Platt scaling outperforms temperature scaling because it can simultaneously correct both overconfidence ($a < 1$) and systematic bias ($b \neq 0$), whereas temperature scaling can only correct overconfidence.

We do not minimize ECE directly because (i) the binning operation is non-differentiable, (ii) ECE is sensitive to the number of bins, and (iii) NLL is a strictly proper scoring rule---its global optimum is achieved if and only if predicted probabilities equal true probabilities---while ECE can be zero even with poorly separated predictions.

\paragraph{Results.}
Figure~\ref{fig:calibration_val} shows reliability diagrams before and after calibration for both models. Platt scaling reduces ECE from $0.028 \to 0.022$ for text and from $0.042 \to 0.019$ for vision. All main-text results use Platt-scaled confidences.

\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/calibration_val_correction.pdf}
    \caption{\textbf{Effect of post-hoc calibration.} Reliability diagrams for text (left) and vision (right) comparing uncalibrated, temperature-scaled, and Platt-scaled confidences. Vision is overconfident before calibration (curve below diagonal); Platt scaling corrects this and yields the lowest ECE for both modalities.}
    \label{fig:calibration_val}
\end{figure}


\section{Data Mixture Ablations}\label{app:data_mixture}

Beyond the configurations reported in Figure~\ref{fig:data-mixture}, we explored several additional training strategies. None consistently outperformed the simple balanced paper-only mixture.

\paragraph{Paper Statistics Features.}
We appended structured metadata---page count, number of references, inline math count, cross-reference density, and other surface-level features---to each training sample. While these features are individually predictive (the tabular logistic baseline reaches $67.2\%$ on ICLR~2025 using 187 such features), adding them to the SFT prompt did not improve over the paper-only baseline, suggesting that the language model already captures these signals implicitly from the paper text.

\paragraph{Thinking Tokens.}
Inspired by chain-of-thought prompting, we prepended dummy reasoning tokens before the decision token during SFT, allowing the model additional computation before committing to a prediction. This did not improve accuracy, consistent with our finding that the decision task benefits more from pattern recognition over the full paper than from explicit multi-step reasoning.

\paragraph{Multimodal Fusion.}
We tested providing both the full extracted text and all page images simultaneously (text + vision), as well as the reverse (vision + text). Neither configuration outperformed the single-modality baselines, likely because the two representations are largely redundant for the decision task and the additional context length increases noise without adding complementary signal.

\paragraph{Synthetic Review Augmentation.}
Appending model-generated reviews (Qwen3.5-120B or Gemini-2.5-Flash) to training prompts added ${\sim}1.8$k--$4.8$k tokens per sample. These reviews occasionally helped on ICLR~2025 but hurt on ICLR~2026, suggesting that review language is more year-specific than paper content and does not transfer reliably.
